= Introduction

LSH-256 and LSH-512 are the hash functions defined in the Korean standard KS X 3262 @kim2014lsh. In Korean cryptographic designs they take the place that SHA-2 usually has elsewhere, for example in PBKDF2 @choi2021pbkdf2 and in the hash-based signature schemes K-XMSS, K-SPHINCS#super[+] @sim2022kxmss and K-LMS @kang2026klms. A hash-based signature calls its hash function thousands of times to produce a single signature, and most of these calls are on short inputs. Because of this, the speed of the signature scheme depends mostly on how fast LSH can hash short messages, and on how many of them it can hash at the same time.

Today there are optimized implementations of LSH in three languages. KISA, the Korea Internet & Security Agency, distributes one in C @kisa2026lsh, the Crypto++ library has one in C++ @dai2026cryptopp, and the go-krypto library has one in Go @ryuanerin2026gokrypto. All three have hand-written SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) code for x86, and the C and Go versions also have SIMD code for ARM. In Rust, LSH has only portable implementations, among them the allcrypt crate @allcrypt2026 and the lsh256 crate @lsh256rs2026.

In this paper we present shimeji, an implementation of LSH-256 and LSH-512 written entirely in Rust, and we compare it with these implementations on an Apple M6 and on an Intel Core i9-13900K. shimeji has two code paths. The first one, the single-message path, hashes one message at a time, which is what every existing implementation does. The second one, the batched path, hashes several independent messages at the same time by putting one message in each SIMD lane. With 32-bit words (LSH-256), a 128-bit NEON register has room for four messages, and with 64-bit words (LSH-512) it has room for two; a 256-bit AVX2 register fits eight and four, respectively. On one message of 1 MiB on the M6, shimeji takes #r2(cpb("m6", "lsh256", 1048576, "shimeji")) cycles per byte for LSH-256 and #r2(cpb("m6", "lsh512", 1048576, "shimeji")) for LSH-512, and is faster than KISA's portable C, the fastest other implementation there, by #factors(best("m6", "lsh256", 1048576).ratio, best("m6", "lsh512", 1048576).ratio). On the i9 it takes #r2(cpb("i9", "lsh256", 1048576, "shimeji")) and #r2(cpb("i9", "lsh512", 1048576, "shimeji")) cycles per byte, and is faster than the fastest other implementation by #factors(best("i9", "lsh256", 1048576).ratio, best("i9", "lsh512", 1048576).ratio).

The idea of batching messages across lanes is not ours. Gueron and Krasnov used it for SHA-1 and SHA-2 in 2012 @gueron2012simultaneous, and it has already been applied to LSH-256 on NEON @bang2026batched and on AVX-512 @lee2026avx512, as we describe in @related. Our contributions are:
- an implementation of LSH-256 and LSH-512 in pure Rust, with single-message and batched paths for NEON and AVX2, which we checked against KISA's code and against the Crypto++ test vectors;
- a single-message path for ARM in which the integer units execute the steps while NEON computes the message expansion, based on the hybrid scalar/vector design that Becker and Kannwischer used for Keccak @becker2022hybrid;
- batched LSH-256 and LSH-512 for AVX2. As far as we know, batched LSH had not been implemented with AVX2 before, and batched LSH-512 had not been implemented on any x86 extension;
- a two-message LSH-512 batch for NEON that is faster than sequential C. It uses XAR, the rotate-and-XOR instruction from the ARMv8.2 SHA-3 extension. If the step constants are rotated beforehand, one XAR instruction does both the rotation and the XOR with the constant;
- a comparison between LSH implementations in C, C++, Go and Rust, on both machines, measured in cycles per byte and in instructions per byte. We did not find a comparison like this in previous work.
